  "chunk": "kill someone), and should continuously reflect on their own strategies, i.e., show\nsome level of metacognition. As Montfort [25] states in his analysis of interactive\nfiction, these games reveal the sophisticated interplay between language com-\nprehension, spatial reasoning, planning, and problem-solving that characterizes\nhuman intelligence.\nThis in stark contrast with the concept of Next Token Prediction (NTP)\nthat has driven recent LLM successes. While an LLM might generate fluent\ntext responses based on statistical patterns in its training data,Zorkarguably\ndemands genuine comprehension: understanding that “the door is locked” im-\nplies the need for a key, that “the room is dark” requires a light source, or that\ncertain puzzles have prerequisites that must be solved first. The question we\naddress in this paper is whether contemporary LLMs can exhibit the flexible,\ngoal-oriented problem-solving that text adventure games likeZorkdemands, or",
